STTY(1)                        P32W Unix                         STTY(1)

NAME
       stty - set terminal modes and size

SYNOPSIS
       stty [mode ...] [rows n] [columns n]
       stty size

DESCRIPTION
       stty sets the modes of the terminal that is its standard
       input, one of /dev/tty0 and /dev/tty1. With no arguments it
       prints the current modes, for example

              echo crmod erase ^H kill ^U

       (a control character is shown as ^ and a letter, DEL as ^?).

       The modes stay set after stty exits, until changed again.
       Each mode is turned on by its name and off by the name with
       a - in front:

       echo   echo each character typed (normally on)
       crmod  on output, send newline as carriage return and
              newline; on input, take a carriage return as a
              newline (normally on)
       cbreak hand characters to the program as they are typed,
              not a line at a time; erase, kill and ^D are not
              special, but ^C and ^\ still send signals (normally
              off)
       raw    hand characters over as typed, all 8 bits, with no
              special characters at all: no signals, no erase or
              kill, no echo, no carriage return mapping on input
              (normally off)
       sane   the normal modes: -raw -cbreak echo crmod

       The system keeps a terminal's size for the programs that
       want it (ls, more, vi, top: ttysize(3)). It knows the
       video screen's, and a window's under wm(1); of a terminal
       on a line it knows nothing until it is told:

       rows n, columns n (or cols n)
              the terminal has n lines, n columns. 0: not known
              (programs then go by LINES and COLUMNS, or 24 by
              80). A program that listens is told of the change
              (SIGWINCH, signal(2)).
       size   print the size, lines then columns: 24 80

       With no arguments the size is printed after the modes when
       it is known: rows 24 columns 80.

       In the normal (cooked) mode input is collected a line at a
       time and these characters are special:

       ^H or DEL
              erase the last character
       ^U     erase (kill) the whole line
       ^D     end of file: the line so far is passed on without a
              newline; at the start of a line a program reads 0
              bytes
       ^C     interrupt: send SIGINT to the terminal's processes
              and discard pending input
       ^\     quit: send SIGQUIT, likewise

EXAMPLES
       stty                          show the modes
       stty -echo                    stop echoing (stty echo undoes)
       stty raw -echo                characters one at a time
       stty -raw echo                back to normal
       stty sane                     the same
       stty rows 40 columns 132      a big terminal on a line

EXIT STATUS
       0 on success; 1 if the standard input is not a terminal
       ("stty: the standard input is not a terminal"); 2 for an
       unknown mode or a size that is not a number from 0 to 999,
       in which case nothing is changed.

SEE ALSO
       sh(1), vi(1), more(1), kermit(1), getty(8), ioctl(2),
       ttysize(3)

BUGS
       Only the four modes above (and sane) and the size exist.
       The speed (always 9600 baud), parity and the special
       characters cannot be changed; erase and kill are shown, but
       stty cannot set them.

       raw still does crmod's newline mapping on output while crmod
       is on. The standard input must be the terminal to be set;
       there is no -f device.
